return [====[patch becomes a thin solid ring for another 0.8 seconds before turning thick and dangerous for 1.2 seconds. Leave the circles or wait; chasing directly along the trail is risky. Unreached points never become damaging patches.</td></tr></table><p>Each whole commitment can hit a given Hero at most once, for physical damage under ordinary defenses. These enemies choose routes with escape space; they cannot carry hazards through locked gates or unsupported floor. Their paths stay fixed and their hazards expire.</p><p>Interrupting, defeating or holding the source cancels the movement and every associated patch. Muted does not stop these physical actions. Cover blocks damage. Dashed plans, solid warnings, thick danger boundaries and shrinking countdowns remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 61</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-perception" data-title="Footsteps and sightlines"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Footsteps and sightlines</h2><table><tr><td>Listener</td><td>The amber Metrocop hears non-crouching footsteps in its own room. Its paired-ring marker and dashed route show a brief investigation toward that sound. Crouch, stop making footsteps, or change course after the cue. It does not track your hidden movement.</td></tr><tr><td>Shy</td><td>The violet fast zombie remembers a visible Hero, then advances toward that last-seen position only when no eligible Hero has line of sight to it. Its crossed-eye marker announces the fixed route. Keep a clear sightline, use a teammate to watch the lane, or relocate behind cover. Camera direction does not matter.</td></tr></table><p>Investigations warn for 0.8 seconds, travel at most 128 units, and end within another 1.4 seconds. Movement itself deals no damage. Listener needs fresh hearing and clear sight to strike; Shy can strike a visible Hero who comes close. Each melee attack has a separate 0.9-second, fixed narrow arc: leave it, use cover, or interrupt.</p><p>Their memory is brief and confined to a supported room. Invisibility prevents acquisition; they cannot follow you through walls, closed gates or missing floor. Interrupting or holding them cancels investigation; Muted does not silence physical footsteps or attacks. The dashed route, distinct destination glyph and countdown remain visible with reduced effects.</p><p>Listener is deliberately footstep-sensitive: gunfire and generic sound effects do not feed its hearing. A crouching Hero can still be threatened by its ordinary companions. Shy remains visible; its rule never forces you to aim or look in a particular direction.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 62</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-conditions" data-title="Cure the ailment, break the bargain"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Cure the ailment, break the bargain</h2><table><tr><td>Absolver</td><td>The pale-cyan slave Vortigaunt draws a crossed-diamond tether to an ailing ally and channels for 1.5 seconds. It removes one existing negative condition, without restoring HP. Interrupt or mute the Absolver, separate it from its ally, or break their sightline. A cure that has already happened cannot be taken back.</td></tr><tr><td>Exactor</td><td>The crimson Combine elite marks an ailing Hero’s position with a 64-unit circle and crossed diamond for 1.25 seconds. Leave the circle, cure the selected Bleeding, Immolated or Poisoned condition, break sight, or interrupt it. The mark never follows you. A successful physical hit consumes that ailment; ordinary Block and Dodge still apply. With no qualifying ailment, Exactor fires an ordinary warned shot.</td></tr></table><p>Absolver accompanies Shamblers; Exactor appears with a Flamer. Avoiding the Flamer’s Immolated attempt deprives Exactor of that opportunity. Exactor threatens only the Hero it marked, and each warning permits at most one hit.</p><p>Cleansing targets one condition in a fixed order, not every ailment. If that condition ends during either warning, the commitment fails; a new application needs a fresh warning. Neither enemy applies a new condition or creates extra bodies.</p><p>Held allows these stationary actions. Muted interrupts Absolver’s cleansing Magic but does not prevent either enemy’s physical shots. The tether, glyphs and shrinking countdowns remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 63</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-spacing" data-title="Together — or apart?"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Together — or apart?</h2><table><tr><td>Outrider</td><td>The sand-colored antlion singles out an isolated Hero. Its lone bracket glyph and narrow, frozen melee arc warn for 1.1 seconds. Bring an eligible teammate within 192 units on the same floor with clear sight to cancel the attack; alone, leave its arc, retreat or interrupt. The strike reaches 112 units and deals physical 1d6+2 before ordinary defenses.</td></tr><tr><td>Conductor</td><td>The pale-blue Vortigaunt marks exactly two nearby Heroes with linked 64-unit circles for 1.4 seconds. Separate beyond 160 units, leave either frozen circle, break its sight to either Hero or break the pair’s sightline to cancel the whole link. Each marked Hero risks one Raw Magic 1d6+2 hit. With no eligible pair, it fires an ordinary warned Raw projectile that a solo Hero can evade.</td></tr></table><p>Both attacks commit to the original Heroes and positions; replacement Heroes and later arrivals do not inherit a warning. No attack tracks hidden movement or adds a lasting field. Outrider recovers for 2.4 seconds after its attack.</p><p>Held permits these stationary attacks. Muted cancels Conductor’s Magic; Outrider’s physical strike remains available. Interrupting or defeating the source cancels its pending warning. The isolation glyph, arc, paired circles, linking line and countdowns remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 64</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-resources" data-title="Protect your Magic — interrupt theirs"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Protect your Magic — interrupt theirs</h2><table><tr><td>Siphoner</td><td>The violet Stalker marks a Hero’s position with a fixed 64-unit circle, downward funnel and 1.25-second countdown. Leave the circle, break its sight or interrupt or mute the caster. A hit deals Raw Magic 1d6+2 through ordinary defenses. Only a surviving Hero who actually loses HP also loses up to 12 current Magic; the Siphoner gains none. A fully absorbed, blocked or dodged hit has no additional drain. It recovers for 3.5 seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>Accumulator</td><td>The gold slave Vortigaunt marks a Hero with a fixed 64-unit circle and lightning glyph for 1.25 seconds. Its Raw Magic 1d6+2 attack spends 40 Magic before its ordinary cost modifiers. When it cannot afford that attack, a battery-and-plus glyph over its own position announces a two-second self-recharge instead. A completed recharge restores up to 45 Magic, capped at 100; interrupt or mute it, force it to move or break its sight to cancel the refill. It recovers for 2.5 seconds.</td></tr></table><p>Both resource attack marks threaten only the original Hero and never follow movement. Leave the circle or break sight before release; later arrivals and replacement lives do not inherit the mark. An Accumulator with an ordinary cast-triggered Aura Burst feat can also deal supplemental aura damage to other nearby Heroes, including those who escaped its circle. That separate feat never adds a Magic-drain rider. Arcane Shield and other defenses keep their ordinary behavior. These enemies do not steal loot, currency or permanent stats.</p><p>The Accumulator’s recharge creates no damaging circle and needs no ally: its battery glyph and countdown expose an opportunity to pressure it. Canceling a recharge still leaves its recovery period. Held permits stationary attacks and recharging; Muted cancels both enemies’ Magic. The drain, lightning and battery shapes and shrinking countdowns remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 65</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-crossfire" data-title="Turn their fire against them"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Turn their fire against them</h2><table><tr><td>Fusilier</td><td>The copper Combine soldier freezes a narrow firing lane for 1.25 seconds. Its arrow and body-bracket glyph warn where the single physical shot will go. Sidestep or place an ordinary enemy in the lane: the first body intercepts the shot, including one of its companions. Walls absorb it. The Fusilier then recovers for three seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>Bombardier</td><td>The ochre Combine elite marks a fixed 72-unit circle with a fragment glyph and 1.6-second countdown. Leave the circle and lure ordinary enemies into it: the physical blast can hit them too. A body cannot shield you from this area attack, but solid cover can. The circle stays where it was placed when you move away. The Bombardier then recovers for 3.5 seconds.</td></tr></table><p>Both attacks deal physical 1d6+2 before ordinary defenses and threaten only actors present when the warning began. Later arrivals and replacement lives do not inherit damage; an unmarked body can still absorb the Fusilier shot harmlessly. Neither enemy harms itself. Named bosses, their clones, event actors, friendly summons and human-controlled Soldiers are excluded from this friendly fire.</p><p>Interrupt, defeat or displace the attacker, or break its sight to cancel the warning. Held and Muted do not stop these stationary physical attacks; morale flight and hit-stun do. The firing lane, blast circle, distinct glyphs and countdowns remain visible with reduced effects. These two enemies do not make the rest of the prison fight among themselves.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 66</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-discipline" data-title="Stop — or keep moving"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Stop — or keep moving</h2><table><tr><td>Halter</td><td>The amber Combine elite tethers one Hero with an octagonal pause glyph and the literal command STOP. During the final 0.4 seconds, keep voluntary speed below one quarter of your legitimate walking speed.</td></tr><tr><td>Pacer</td><td>The cyan Metrocop tethers one Hero with double chevrons and KEEP MOVING. During the final 0.4 seconds, maintain voluntary speed at or above one quarter of your legitimate walking speed.</td></tr></table><p>Each demand warns for 1.6 seconds. PREPARE becomes JUDGMENT for the final 0.4 seconds; the countdown, thicker glyph and quarter-bar bracket identify that interval. A violating observation during judgment commits one physical 1d6+2 hit at release, subject to ordinary defenses. Comply throughout the interval to avoid it. Earlier movement does not decide the result.</p><p>The tether follows only the originally captured Hero life. Only one demand can hold a Hero at a time, including across both enemy types. Break sight with solid cover, leave the 360-unit range or current cell, or interrupt or defeat the source to cancel the demand. Later arrivals and replacement lives never inherit it.</p><p>Only actual voluntary motion counts: movement input against a wall is not enough for KEEP MOVING. Forced pushes, Held, nonwalking movement or stale motion evidence cancel the demand harmlessly, rather than making a forced failure count. An interval without enough reliable movement evidence also cancels.</p><p>Held and Muted on the attacker permit these stationary physical demands; morale flight and hit-stun cancel them. STOP and KEEP MOVING text, distinct glyphs, tether and countdown remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 67</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-companions" data-title="Break the bodyguard and the oath"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Break the bodyguard and the oath</h2><table><tr><td>Interposer</td><td>The blue Combine elite warns BODYGUARD for 0.8 seconds, then moves its own body along the frozen route toward an ally’s firing line. Its shield glyph stays on its actual body. Flank, change angle, use piercing or area attacks, interrupt, or defeat it. The route never follows your new position; its arrival hold lasts at most two seconds.</td></tr><tr><td>Mourner</td><td>The violet Metrocop warns OATH for 0.8 seconds, then binds one ordinary ally for three seconds. If that exact ally is legitimately defeated during the armed oath, the broken-oath glyph and RETALIATION announce a separate, full 1.2-second shot warning against the original Hero. Prioritize the Mourner, break the tether, wait out the oath, or defeat its ally and evade the warned shot.</td></tr></table><p>The Interposer grants no Block, immunity or damage diversion: ordinary body interception alone can protect the ward, so protection is never guaranteed. Held prevents its movement. The Mourner grants no resurrection or extra rewards; an old corpse cannot trigger its oath. The killer does not become a new retaliation target.</p><p>Without an eligible ally, either enemy warns SHOT for 1.2 seconds. Every fallback or retaliation shot freezes the original Hero’s visible position when that shot warning begins, then releases one physical 1d6+2 packet before ordinary defenses. Leave the fixed lane, use cover or another body, or interrupt the attacker. The first actual body or wall blocks the shot; an intervening enemy is not damaged by it. Each attack ends in three seconds of recovery.</p><p>Only one bodyguard or oath can reserve an ordinary ally at a time. Breaking sight, leaving range, displacing the stationary source or ward, or defeating the source cancels the commitment. Held permits stationary oaths and shots; Muted permits these physical actions. Morale flight and hit-stun cancel them. BODYGUARD, OATH, RETALIATION and SHOT labels, route or lane, distinct shield or oath glyphs and countdowns remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 68</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-edicts" data-title="Cease fire or find refuge"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Cease fire or find refuge</h2><table><tr><td>Censor</td><td>The ochre Combine soldier marks one Hero with a crossed-barrel glyph and CEASE FIRE. PREPARE lasts 0.8 seconds; WATCH lasts 2.4 seconds. Starting a firearm, crowbar, Magic or Wand attack during WATCH triggers a separate, full 1.2-second RETALIATION warning. Stop attacking through WATCH, interrupt or defeat the Censor, break sight with solid cover, or leave range.</td></tr><tr><td>Surveyor</td><td>The mint Vortigaunt freezes a 144-unit threat ring around your initial position for 1.6 seconds. REFUGE marks a safe pocket of radius 48, displaced 96 units from the ring center. Enter that pocket or LEAVE RING before judgment. Both routes are checked for your actual body when the warning starts. The rings and arrows stay fixed when you move.</td></tr></table><p>The Censor observes new committed attacks, not damage: passive effects, ailments, earlier projectiles and equipment actions that do not commit an attack cannot trigger it. Retaliation freezes your visible position when its new warning begins. Leave the fixed lane, use a wall or intervening body, or interrupt the source. The first body or solid cover absorbs the shot; an intervening enemy is not damaged. The single physical hit is 1d6+2 before ordinary defenses.</p><p>The Surveyor releases one physical 1d6+2 hit only if the originally marked Hero remains inside the outer ring and outside the refuge at judgment. Solid cover, leaving range, interrupting or defeating the Surveyor also cancels it. Muted cancels Surveyor judgment; Held does not. Other enemies can still threaten either escape route: refuge protects only against this judgment.</p><p>Each commitment belongs to the original Hero life. A death, replacement life, reset or invalid geometry cancels it; nobody else inherits the warning. Held and Muted permit the Censor’s stationary physical actions. Morale flight and hit-stun interrupt both enemies. Three seconds of recovery follow each commitment. Labels, distinct glyphs, fixed geometry and countdowns remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 69</div></article><article class="page" id="enemy-links" data-title="Break the relay and the living ribbon"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Break the relay and the living ribbon</h2><table><tr><td>Relay</td><td>The ice-blue Combine soldier draws a linked-diamond channel to one ordinary ally and a separate arrow from that ally toward your frozen position. RELAY SHOT warns for 1.4 seconds. The shot comes from the ally’s captured position, so watch the far end of the channel. Sidestep the fixed shot, interpose a body or solid cover, or defeat either endpoint.</td></tr><tr><td>Lacemaker</td><td>The rose Metrocop draws a ground ribbon to one ordinary ally. LEAVE RIBBON warns for 1.2 seconds before the ribbon becomes ACTIVE for two seconds. Its double edge encloses an 18-unit radius. The source stays still while the ally continues its own ordinary movement: bait the ally to rotate the line away, leave the ribbon, break the tether with cover, or defeat either endpoint.</td></tr></table><p>The Relay’s arrow never tracks your new position or the ally’s movement. The first body or solid wall absorbs its single physical 1d6+2 shot before ordinary defenses; intervening enemies receive no damage from it. Moving the ally more than 32 units from its captured position cancels the channel.</p><p>The Lacemaker’s live ribbon can deliver at most one physical 1d6+2 hit to the originally captured Hero, subject to ordinary defenses. Other Heroes and enemies receive no ribbon damage. The ally remains in the same legal room, within 96 units of its starting position; a sudden move, unsupported ground or broken connection cancels the ribbon. There is no invisible lingering trail.</p><p>Without an eligible ally, either enemy displays SOURCE SHOT and a fixed arrow from its own body for a full 1.4 seconds. Leave that lane, use a body or solid cover, or interrupt the source. The shot uses the same ordinary physical defenses and recovery.</p><p>Every commitment belongs to the exact source, original Hero and captured ally lives. Death, replacement life, reset, excessive displacement or invalid geometry cancels it. Held and Muted permit these stationary physical actions; morale flight and hit-stun interrupt the attacker. Neither enemy creates extra bodies or shared health. Three seconds of recovery follow each commitment. Distinct glyphs, endpoint labels, literal instructions and countdowns remain visible with reduced effects.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 70</div></article><article class="page" id="neil" data-title="Neil has the Black Keycard"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Neil has the Black Keycard</h2><div class="boss-art" role="img" aria-label="Neil, Brute, and Gordon the Warden"></div><p>After Yellow Gate, look for Neil: the green-suited bureaucrat clutching a briefcase. Neil flees through the open labyrinth and across connected floors. He does not attack you. He alone carries the Black Keycard.</p><p>Brute is his independently killable antlion-guard escort. It keeps near Neil and reacts when you hit him. Orange warning marks a charge: after a 1.25-second preparation, Brute commits to its direction. Sidestep that line. A wall collision stuns it for two seconds.</p><p>You may defeat the escort first, but Brute is not an invulnerability switch. Neil can be defeated independently. Take the Black Keycard dropped by Neil to progress.</p><p>Neil is fixed at Dungeon Level. Brute is fixed at Dungeon Level +1.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 71</div></article><article class="page" id="gordon" data-title="Gordon the Warden"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Gordon the Warden</h2><div class="boss-art" role="img" aria-label="Neil, Brute, and Gordon the Warden"></div><p>Beyond Black Gate is a two-level arena: a lower court and an upper gallery. Gordon is the heavy man in a pig mask. Entering commits the encounter and closes the back gate.</p><p>An entry or respawn alcove provides protection and one aid allowance per Hero identity per dungeon: up to 25 HP, up to 30 ammunition per owned gun, and one Healing Potion. Repeated entry is not an unlimited supply.</p><p>Above roughly 60% HP, Gordon vanishes and physically moves through the arena for three seconds. A contracting ring marks his departure; a fixed square with inward arrows warns where he will reappear. That destination warning comes before his ordinary visible attack warning and four slow homing orbs. Around 60%, he rides his toilet and leaves bombs with three-second fuses. Around 25%, he clears that ordnance and charges with his crowbar.</p><p>During phase one, the first effective hit in a visible attack appearance or taunt cancels his unreleased shots and leaves a 1.2-second follow-up opportunity. More hits do not extend it, and no continuous appearance can exceed four seconds. Ordinary hit stun cannot pin him while cloaked or reappearing; invisibility itself does not block damage. Shared conditions still apply. Brief contextual taunts replace some attacks, with one speaker at a time and ordinary attack appearances between them. Hitting a taunt ends the gesture but preserves the same follow-up opportunity. Crowbar stagger still works at most once every three seconds; later phases retain their ordinary hit-stun rules.</p><p>Fake Gordon Clones join at Dungeon Levels 4, 8, 12 and 16, up to four. Each has one-third of the real Gordon’s maximum HP. They spread around the arena and have no boss health bar. Defeating a clone does not release the Jail Key. Defeating the real Gordon ends his fight; at Level 20 it reveals Hector instead of releasing the key.</p><p>A living, deployed Hero can spot a fake at up to half their current Wisdom bonus in squares, never below zero. Half-squares count; a +3 bonus reaches 1.5 squares. You need a clear view and must actually be that close, including height. In that range a fake has a slight greenish tint, sticks out its tongue, and periodically winks either eye. Its normal hit stun has an exaggerated sideways recoil, without any extra stun time. These tells are personal: a wiser teammate does not reveal them for you, and they disappear with distance, cover or cloaking. Real Gordon gets none of these tells. Zero or negative Wisdom bonus grants no recognition range.</p><p>Corner Sentry turrets join Gordon at Dungeon Levels 5, 10, 15 and 20: one, two, three, then four. Four is the limit even in endless cash runs; their ordinary monster strength still grows with the dungeon. Each appears in a separately seeded, clear lower-court corner pocket. A blocked or unsafe corner is skipped for that fight, not replaced later. Destroyed turrets do not respawn when a Hero dies or rejoins.</p><p>A turret faces inward with a narrow frontal firing cone. Its glowing muzzle and straight warning line give at least three-quarters of a second before one fast, nonhoming bullet. Sidestep the frozen line, use real cover, flank it, interrupt its windup or destroy it. The safe entry alcove works in both directions: you cannot shoot a turret from safety. Both stairs and the gallery remain routes around the fight. Surviving turrets and their attacks retire when Gordon falls, before Hector appears at Level 20.</p><p>A Damsel&#x27;s Revenge consumable can arm the jailed Damsel during this fight. She provides stationary support against the real Gordon only; the item chapter explains her firing slit and the owner-only gun pickup after normal rescue.</p><p>The fight does not reset just because a Hero dies. At Levels 1–19 and 21 onward, defeat Gordon, take the Jail Key at the arena center, open the rescue chamber, and reach the damsel—or the cash after Level 20. At Level 20, defeat Hector first. Gordon’s death alone never completes the dungeon.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 72</div></article><article class="page" id="hector" data-title="Hector the Director"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Hector the Director</h2><table><tr><td>Level 20 only</td><td>Gordon → Hector → Jail Key → Deborah</td></tr><tr><td>Your target</td><td>The Director’s Heart in the court</td></tr></table><p>When the real Gordon falls at Level 20, an enormous, disturbingly familiar figure rises over the Flattywood horizon. Hector’s three-second reveal begins the final fight. The jail remains locked until you defeat him.</p><p>Attack the Director’s Heart: the red figure at the arena center, tethered to the giant above. This reachable core takes your ordinary weapon and spell hits. The distant body is a spectacle, not a separate target. Fight from the lower court or its upper gallery; the protected entrance permits neither outgoing nor incoming Hector damage.</p><p>Hector opens with blue missile volleys and titanic crowbar strikes. Below roughly 60% health, bombs and purple corrupted Villain of Lore bolts enter the rotation. Below roughly 25%, attacks arrive faster and ground strikes may mark several Heroes. Watch the warning text, leave fixed ground circles before impact, and dodge the traveling orbs.</p><p>Death and revival use the normal Hero rules. Returning Heroes resume the same fight; Hector does not heal or restart because everyone temporarily leaves the court. His attacks stop while no visible, living Hero is available there. The dungeon’s collapse clock still matters.</p><p>Hector grants ordinary combat credit and enemy drops, with no separate currency prize. After his defeat, take the Jail Key, open Deborah’s chamber, and rescue her to finish Level 20. The existing Level 21-and-beyond SECURE THE BAG campaign then continues.</p><p>The familiar Hermit is revealed as Hector the Director. Only after his defeat and your successful rescue of Deborah does the finite rescue story end. Deborah succeeds him in staging; the campaign continues into SECURE THE BAG.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 73</div></article><article class="page" id="finale" data-title="The rescue story is won"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>The rescue story is won</h2><p>Deborah’s legitimate rescue begins a 6.5-second celebration inside the normal 20-second victory window. The rescued damsels cheer, and surviving participating Heroes receive her thanks and affectionate celebration beats. Your rescue rewards settle immediately; the celebration grants no extra reward.</p><p>The opening announces Deborah’s rescue, followed by the Hero celebration, then THE RESCUE STORY IS WON. Your actual Hero stays in place. Movement briefly pauses for the participating survivors, then returns. F can open the usual optional Tetris throughout the victory window and dismisses the finale view.</p><p>Reduced effects keeps your ordinary view and the story titles. If you join late, you see the current moment. Missing actors, a death or a reconnect cannot restart the celebration or pay the rescue again.</p><p>At the usual end of the victory window, return to staging for Dungeon 21. Deborah occupies the former Hermit’s position and offers Abundance through the same once-per-account, rolling 24-hour claim. Prepare your equipment, then use the portal to SECURE THE BAG.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 74</div></article><article class="page" id="fund" data-title="The Debbie Fund"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>The Debbie Fund</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:0% 0%"></div><p>The Debbie Fund takes rescue funding very seriously. Its $DEB units are Debbie Coins—&#x27;Debs&#x27;—and its collectibles are Debbie Fund Tokens, or DFTs. These are server-local game records, with no cash value or outside wallet.</p><p>A successful rescue creates a pool of 100 × Dungeon Level Debs. Qualifying Soldier-attributable Hero life losses assign 11% each to the Soldier side, capped at 33% of the pool; Soldier damage contributions determine its allocation.</p><p>The remaining Hero pool is divided half equally and half by damage and effective allied healing, with hostile-loss adjustments. Eligible dead or disconnected participants can retain their claim. Switching to Soldier play forfeits a Hero share.</p><p>Rescue awards increase wallet balance and lifetime rescue earnings. DFT sales increase spendable balance but do not inflate that lifetime rescue score. The staging Heroes of Legend board records the highest dungeon reached, damsels rescued, and cash bags recovered; it is not a count of monster kills.</p><p>At Debbie, choose Fuse Equipment, Sell Equipment, Fuse DFTs, or Sell DFTs. Drag eligible items into the labeled pile, review the value, and confirm. You can also click items to add or remove them. Closing the menu cancels staging without moving anything. Sales award the displayed value in $DEB. Fusion consumes two to eight inputs and returns one legal item worth 85–100% of their combined value and more than any single input; unsupported piles are rejected intact. Equipment fusion returns carried gear; DFT fusion returns a persistent upgraded token. DFT-created gear and free starting weapons cannot be sold or fused. If any fused DFT was recreated this run, the new token remains used for this run. DFT Collection / Recreate retains the free recreation service.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 75</div></article><article class="page" id="tokens" data-title="A little something to keep"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A little something to keep</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:100% 0%"></div><p>DFTs normally mint when a dungeon objective is completed. Combat Level milestones 1, 5, 10, and 20 can each award one token per account on this server. A rare rescue roll also gives each participating Hero a one-in-1,000 chance. Ordinary enemy kills do not mint DFTs. The treasure chest event can also award DFTs; see Treasure to keep. Rescuing Deborah at Level 20 also unlocks Abundance: visit her in staging for one free DFT per Steam account every rolling 24 hours. Reconnecting or restarting a campaign does not reset the timer. The eight-token capacity still applies; a full wallet leaves the gift available until you make room. Recreated equipment retains the usual resale and fusion exclusions.</p><p>Your collection has eight slots. Milestone items waiting for space stay frozen; making room does not reroll them. Tokens persist across ordinary death and campaign restart.</p><p>At the staging Debbie statue, recreate a token&#x27;s frozen item for free once per token per run. The DFT remains in your collection. The same token becomes available to recreate again in the next run.</p><p>You may instead sell a DFT permanently for its displayed Value in Debs. Read the confirmation carefully: selling the token removes it. Wallet viewing is portable; the statue&#x27;s staging, proximity, and role rules still govern its physical services.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 76</div></article><article class="page" id="dungeon-events" data-title="Dungeon Events: discoveries and obstacles"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Dungeon Events: discoveries and obstacles</h2><table><tr><td>Slot machine · $5 DEB stake</td><td>One ordinary d4: 4 pays $15 gross (+$10 net); 1–3 pays nothing (−$5 net).</td></tr><tr><td>Odds</td><td>25% win; 75% loss. No exploding dice or ability bonuses.</td></tr></table><p>An enabled dungeon contains 1–4 different event types from a catalog of 28, chosen by one ordinary, non-exploding d4. A 1–3 selects common events; a 4 selects three common events plus one rare opportunity: DFT Treasure, Contraband Countdown or Life Underwriter. Treasure can contain one or two chests but counts once; paired warp holes also count once and begin at Dungeon Level 5. The labyrinth remembers recent discoveries, favors fresh combinations, and places them to suit the surrounding rooms. Events never replace the required rescue objective.</p><p>Debbie Slots is optional and never blocks the rescue route. Read its posted stake and odds before pressing E to play. Each Steam account may complete one play per dungeon; reconnecting or beginning a replacement Hero does not grant another play. Other Heroes have their own attempt.</p><p>The stake uses your persistent, server-local $DEB balance, which has no cash value. A completed play applies the stake and payout together. Insufficient funds or a storage failure spends nothing and leaves your attempt available; retrying does not reroll the outcome. Leaving the dungeon ends access to that machine.</p><p>Events are enabled by default on new server configurations. Servers retaining an earlier disabled setting need an administrator to set lod_events_enabled 1 for future dungeon generation.</p><p>In developer mode, administrators can run lod_event_population_preview to generate a normal 1–4 event selection and print its locations, or lod_event_preview_generate followed by an event name for a single-event preview. Previews mark the campaign unranked but still use carried keys and persistent server-local funds and DFTs; they provide no free currency. Regenerating the same dungeon does not refresh account claims.</p><p>Some opportunities belong to each account, some affect everyone, and some go to the first buyer. Read the offer and ownership before acting. A new dungeon brings new opportunities; reconnecting, replacing a Hero or regenerating the same dungeon never renews a spent claim.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 77</div></article><article class="page" id="prison-services" data-title="Scavenged services"><div class="kicker">Dungeon Events</div><h2>Scavenged services</h2><table><tr><td>Prison Triage</td><td>12 $DEB restores missing HP. It does not cure ailments.</td></tr><tr><td>Blood Dynamo</td><td>Trade 10 HP for up to 25 Magic. You must keep at least 1 HP.</td></tr><tr><td>Salvage Press</td><td>Destroy the reviewed spare wearable for its displayed $DEB value.</td></tr><tr><td>Scavenger Reforge</td><td>Fuse two reviewed spare wearables into the displayed stronger item.</td></tr><tr><td>Improvised Key Cutter</td><td>One Healing Potion and 20 Magic become one Chest Key.</td></tr><tr><td>Ammo Transmuter</td><td>15 Magic buys a large ration for the firearm you are holding.</td></tr><tr><td>Wounded Scavenger</td><td>Exchange one Healing Potion for the displayed wearable.</td></tr><tr><td>Redline Capacitor</td><td>Fill Magic, but become Reckless for 30 seconds: your attacks can harm allies.</td></tr><tr><td>Contraband Countdown</td><td>One shared item. Its price falls while you wait; another Hero can buy it first.</td></tr><tr><td>Life Underwriter</td><td>Sell one reserve life for 40 $DEB. Your final life cannot be sold.</td></tr></table><p>Press Use to review the exact offer. Press Use again within 12 seconds to confirm it. If your inventory, Hero or offer changes, review the refreshed offer before paying. Looking at a machine does not buy anything.</p><p>Each account gets one successful transaction per service per dungeon, except Contraband Countdown, whose one lot goes to the first buyer. Starter and DFT equipment cannot be salvaged or reforged. Insufficient resources, full bags or full stacks leave payment untouched.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 78</div></article><article class="page" id="prison-incidents" data-title="Read the room"><div class="kicker">Dungeon Events</div><h2>Read the room</h2><table><tr><td>Steam Vent</td><td>Four seconds safe, one second warning, one second steam. Within 64 units, a failed WIS save can Mute you for 2 seconds. Seal the valve or walk around.</td></tr><tr><td>Stasis Beacon</td><td>Standing close risks 2 seconds of Clumsy after a failed DEX save. Crouch past it or switch it off.</td></tr><tr><td>Watchful Eye</td><td>Meeting its gaze risks 3 seconds of Reckless after a failed WIS save. Avert your eyes, circle behind the screen and unplug it.</td></tr><tr><td>Prison Surveyor</td><td>Ask for your current objective’s physical floor and first steps along the presently open route.</td></tr><tr><td>Silent Archive</td><td>Use, then stay grounded, motionless and quiet for 4 seconds to restore up to 25 Magic. Moving cancels freely.</td></tr></table><p>The three machines impose ordinary saving throws and existing conditions. Their effects are avoidable; disabling a machine makes it safe for everyone. A disabled machine grants no cash reward. Normal combat continues while you investigate.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 79</div></article><article class="page" id="prison-challenges" data-title="The prison plays games"><div class="kicker">Dungeon Events</div><h2>The prison plays games</h2><table><tr><td>Counterweight Cache</td><td>Two Heroes hold opposite plates for 3 seconds. Alone, use the central lever and hold still for 10 seconds. Once open, each account collects 15 $DEB.</td></tr><tr><td>Prison Relay</td><td>Run across numbered markers 1, 2 and 3 in order within 12 seconds, then collect 15 $DEB at the console.</td></tr><tr><td>Memory Terminal</td><td>Watch three numbered lights, then use the corresponding buttons in order for 15 $DEB. You have 30 seconds; mistakes let you try again without charge.</td></tr><tr><td>Nerve Clock</td><td>Use to start, then use again during green for 20 $DEB. Red spends your attempt and risks 3 seconds of Reckless. The cycle is 3 seconds red, 1 green.</td></tr><tr><td>Strength Press</td><td>One non-exploding d20 plus your STR modifier against 12. Success pays 20 $DEB; failure spends the attempt without charging you.</td></tr></table><p>Numbered markers and personal prompts show the current challenge. Each successful reward belongs to your account once per dungeon; other Heroes have their own claims. Nerve Clock and Strength Press also remember unsuccessful results.</p><p>These games do not pause the dungeon or protect you from enemies. Let a friend watch the corridor. Counterweight’s solo lever means every challenge remains usable without a second human.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 80</div></article><article class="page" id="locked-chest" data-title="A key, or a Rogue’s touch"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A key, or a Rogue’s touch</h2><table><tr><td>Chest Key</td><td>Bag-only consumable; stacks up to nine. Press E at a locked chest to use one.</td></tr><tr><td>Rogue lockpick</td><td>Press E without a key, or hold Sprint + E to pick while carrying keys.</td></tr><tr><td>Success chance</td><td>5% per Combat Level, up to 95%; one ordinary d100 roll.</td></tr></table><p>The locked loot chest sits off the required rescue route and offers each Steam account one ordinary procedural wearable per dungeon. It does not award a DFT; the item uses your normal equipment storage.</p><p>Chest Keys can appear among normal loot drops and remain in your bag rather than the Throwable slot. A key is spent only when its chest item successfully enters your equipment storage. A full bag or failed delivery spends no key; make room or retry.</p><p>A Rogue gets one lockpick attempt per chest. Failure uses that attempt but leaves any key intact, and you may later open the chest with a key. A full bag or another rejected preflight does not use the attempt. If a successful pick cannot deliver the item, retry to claim the same reward without another roll or a key.</p><p>The chest remembers your account’s claim and lockpick attempt through reconnects, replacement Heroes, and regeneration of the same dungeon. Those actions cannot grant another attempt or reroll the reward. A new dungeon provides a new opportunity; a new campaign resets the chest record.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 81</div></article><article class="page" id="treasure-chest" data-title="Treasure to keep"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Treasure to keep</h2><table><tr><td>DFT treasure event</td><td>One ordinary d2 selects one or two optional chests; each chest offers each Steam account one DFT.</td></tr><tr><td>Collection capacity</td><td>Eight DFTs. Ordinary equipment bag space does not limit this reward.</td></tr><tr><td>Opening the lock</td><td>Use a Chest Key, or use the same Rogue lockpicking controls and chance as an ordinary locked chest.</td></tr></table><p>Treasure chests hold persistent Debbie Fund Tokens, not ordinary equipment pickups. Each Hero has an independent claim at each chest. The reward is fixed: reconnecting, replacing your Hero, or regenerating the same dungeon cannot reroll it or restore a claim. Selling or recreating the awarded DFT does not reopen the chest.</p><p>Press E to spend one Chest Key when the DFT is successfully delivered. A Rogue without a key may pick the lock; hold Sprint + E to choose lockpicking while carrying keys. Each account gets one attempt per chest at 5% per Combat Level, up to 95%. A failed pick consumes the attempt but no key; you can return with a key.</p><p>A full DFT collection spends neither key nor lockpick attempt. Make room in your collection, then return. A storage failure spends no key. If a successful pick cannot deliver its DFT, the chest stays unlocked for your account: retry for the same reward without another roll or key.</p><p>Treasure events appear only on a four-event selection and contain one or two optional chests. Their DFTs persist in your server-local collection and have no cash value. Leaving the dungeon ends access to its chests. For an administrator preview, use lod_event_preview_generate treasure_chest; lod_chest_key_testkit supplies one key only if you have none.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 82</div></article><article class="page" id="vending-machine" data-title="A bottle for the road"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A bottle for the road</h2><table><tr><td>Healing Potion · $10 DEB</td><td>One bottle per Steam account per campaign dungeon. Each Hero has an independent purchase.</td></tr><tr><td>Potion effect</td><td>Restores 25 HP and cures negative statuses when used. Buying the bottle does not heal you.</td></tr><tr><td>Carrying capacity</td><td>Healing Potions stack up to three and use the Throwable slot.</td></tr></table><p>Press E at a vending machine to buy one standard Healing Potion for your equipment inventory. The price uses your persistent, server-local $DEB balance, which has no cash value. The potion is ordinary run equipment, not a DFT.</p><p>Insufficient funds, a full potion stack, a storage failure, or leaving the interaction before it can complete spends nothing and leaves your purchase available. Correct the problem and retry. A successful purchase charges exactly $10 DEB and delivers one bottle.</p><p>The machine remembers your account’s completed purchase through death, replacement Heroes, reconnects, and regeneration of the same dungeon. Drinking or discarding the bottle does not restock the machine, and reconnecting does not replace a lost bottle. A new dungeon or campaign provides a new purchase.</p><p>This optional machine can appear alongside other Dungeon Events. For an administrator preview, use lod_event_preview_generate vending_machine. Purchases spend real server-local $DEB; the preview does not provide free currency.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 83</div></article><article class="page" id="false-floor" data-title="Watch your step"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Watch your step</h2><p>A False-floor hazard is a small panel in an optional upper-floor room. Stepping fully onto it as a grounded Hero can open the floor beneath you. There is no E prompt to activate it. The solid rim around the panel gives you room to go around.</p><p>The fall leads one floor down. Ordinary gravity and falling consequences apply. The destination preserves your current progression access, and the existing stairs provide a route back; the trap never opens a locked progression shortcut.</p><p>The panel stays open for at least three seconds. It closes and rearms only after its opening is clear, so keep moving after landing. All Heroes share the same panel state, and the hazard can trigger again. If the landing is unsafe or occupied before activation, the panel stays closed.</p><p>For an administrator preview, use lod_event_preview_generate false_floor. Redeploy after generation and follow the printed locator.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 84</div></article><article class="page" id="warp-hole" data-title="A shorter way back"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>A shorter way back</h2><p>From Dungeon Level 5 onward, a paired warp hole may appear as an optional event. Its two cyan endpoints sit on different physical floors. The prompt identifies your endpoint and its destination floor.</p><p>Press E at either dormant endpoint. Your first successful traversal links the pair for everyone until this dungeon ends. Linked holes are free and repeatable; wait one second between your own traversals. Other Heroes can use them independently.</p><p>A warp hole shortens a route you can already reach. It never skips a locked gate, required boss, key or rescue step, and the ordinary stairs still provide a way back. It preserves your facing and stops your previous momentum.</p><p>If the arrival is occupied or unsafe, you stay where you are. Clear the landing and try again. A refused first traversal leaves the pair dormant. Regeneration replaces both endpoints with a fresh dormant pair.</p><p>For an administrator preview, use lod_event_preview_generate warp_hole. This unranked single-event preview can show the pair before Dungeon Level 5. Redeploy after generation and follow the printed locators.</p><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 85</div></article><article class="page" id="tips" data-title="Deborah&#x27;s parting advice"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Deborah&#x27;s parting advice</h2><div role="img" aria-label="Deborah demonstrates" class="art" style="background-size:300% 200%;background-position:50% 100%"></div><ol><li>The clock pauses in staging and starts when the first Hero deploys. Every new dungeon gives you thirty minutes.</li><li>Keep a useful spell selected. A bottle in your hand means you are holding a bottle—not casting.</li><li>Move sideways out of committed charges. A longer corridor is not always a safer escape.</li><li>Compare gear drawbacks as carefully as benefits. Keep your permanent feat requirements in mind.</li><li>Use short map checks, read the current objective, and remember the stairs.</li><li>When the Warden falls, finish the job. At Level 20, defeat Hector too. Then Jail Key, jail door, rescue target.</li></ol><blockquote>“You&#x27;ve read the book. Now make yourself useful.”<br>— Deborah</blockquote><div class="folio">THE LEGEND OF DEBORAH · 86</div></article><article class="page" id="skeleton-blockade" data-title="Skeleton of a Hero"><div class="kicker">How to play</div><h2>Skeleton of a Hero</h2><table><tr><td>Class</td><td>Fighter, Rogue or Wizard, chosen when the dungeon is built.</td></tr><tr><td>Combat Level</td><td>Dungeon Level + 2, capped at 20.</td></tr><tr><td>Passage</td><td>Defeat the Skeleton once; the route opens for everyone.</td></tr></table><p>Skeleton Blockades are frequent encounters from Dungeon Level 1. After two completed dungeons with events but no Skeleton, the next event selection includes one. The other event slots and rare-event rules still apply.</p><p>A Skeleton of a Hero guards a required passage. Its name begins “Skeleton of” and comes from the same name generator as your Hero. Its abilities, identity perks, class growth, health dice and eligible automatic feats use the ordinary H]====]
